<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>Self-study quiz.</strong> This quiz is for your own review — answers are not recorded. You’ll get instant feedback on each question.</p>
</div></div>

<div class="quiz"><div class="quiz__top-bar"><div class="quiz__score-tracker" style="display:none;"></div><button class="quiz__start-over" type="button" style="display:none;">Start Over</button></div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q1b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">1</span> <div><p>In the <em>Euthyphro</em>, Socrates asks Euthyphro a question that has become one of the most important in the philosophy of religion. Which of the following best states that question?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1a" id="q1a">
        <label for="q1a">Is piety the same as justice, or are they different virtues?</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1b" id="q1b">
        <label for="q1b">Is something pious because the gods love it, or do the gods love it because it is pious?</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1c" id="q1c">
        <label for="q1c">Can the gods disagree about what is pious, or must they all agree?</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1d" id="q1d">
        <label for="q1d">Is piety a matter of action, or a matter of intention?</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. This question — known as the Euthyphro dilemma — forces a choice between two unsatisfactory positions and exposes the central problem with Divine Command Theory.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Not quite. Socrates' key question asks about the direction of explanation between piety and divine love — which one depends on the other? Review the Euthyphro lecture page, Section 3.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q2b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">2</span> <div><p>According to Socrates' critique, what is the main problem with the view that something is pious <em>because</em> the gods love it?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2a" id="q2a">
        <label for="q2a">It makes piety dependent on which gods you worship, which is culturally relative.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2b" id="q2b">
        <label for="q2b">It makes piety entirely arbitrary — anything at all could be pious if the gods happened to love it.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2c" id="q2c">
        <label for="q2c">It makes piety impossible to know, since we cannot directly observe what the gods love.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2d" id="q2d">
        <label for="q2d">It makes the gods responsible for human evil, which is theologically unacceptable.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. If piety just is whatever the gods love, then piety has no independent standard — torturing innocents would be pious if the gods loved it. Socrates and Euthyphro both find this unacceptable.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Socrates' central objection is that grounding piety in divine love makes piety arbitrary — there is no independent standard of rightness. Review the Euthyphro lecture page, Section 3.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q3b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">3</span> <div><p>Euthyphro's first answer to "What is piety?" is that piety is what he is doing right now — prosecuting his father for wrongdoing. Why does Socrates reject this answer?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3a" id="q3a">
        <label for="q3a">Because prosecuting one's own father is impious, not pious.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3b" id="q3b">
        <label for="q3b">Because it gives only one example of a pious act rather than explaining what makes any act pious.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3c" id="q3c">
        <label for="q3c">Because Euthyphro's father may not actually be guilty of wrongdoing.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3d" id="q3d">
        <label for="q3d">Because piety and justice are different virtues and should not be confused.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Socrates wants a definition — something the same in all and only pious things — not an example. Pointing to one instance of piety does not tell us what makes any act pious. This is also a case of begging the question: Euthyphro assumes the very concept he is supposed to define.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Socrates is not disputing whether Euthyphro's action is pious. He wants to know what piety itself is — a general account, not a specific example. Review Section 3 of the Euthyphro lecture page.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q4b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">4</span> <div><p>Which of the following best describes how the Socratic elenchus works?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4a" id="q4a">
        <label for="q4a">Socrates presents a lecture on the correct view, then invites objections from his interlocutor.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4b" id="q4b">
        <label for="q4b">Socrates gets his interlocutor to agree to a series of premises, then shows those premises lead to a contradiction or an absurd conclusion.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4c" id="q4c">
        <label for="q4c">Socrates appeals to the authority of previous philosophers to show that his interlocutor is wrong.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4d" id="q4d">
        <label for="q4d">Socrates offers a definition, invites criticism, and then revises his definition in response.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The elenchus works by drawing out the interlocutor's own commitments and showing they are internally inconsistent. Socrates does not argue from authority or deliver lectures — he argues from agreed premises.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The elenchus is a method of refutation through questioning, not lecturing or appealing to authority. Review Section 2 of the Euthyphro lecture page.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q5b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">5</span> <div><p>The goal of Socratic dialectic is to win an argument by persuading an audience that your position is correct and your opponent's is wrong.</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5a" id="q5a">
        <label for="q5a">True</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5b" id="q5b">
        <label for="q5b">False</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct — this is false. That description fits debate. The goal of dialectic is inquiry: to learn something new about the issue under discussion. Unlike debate, in dialectic your sharpest critic is your best friend.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">That description fits debate, not dialectic. Dialectic is aimed at inquiry and discovery, not victory. Review Section 2 of the Euthyphro lecture page and the Logic Reference.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q6c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">6</span> <div><p>The <em>Euthyphro</em> ends without a definition of piety, and Euthyphro walks away. Which of the following best describes what the dialogue has nevertheless achieved?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6a" id="q6a">
        <label for="q6a">It has proven that piety does not exist and that religious belief is irrational.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6b" id="q6b">
        <label for="q6b">It has shown that the question of piety has no correct answer, confirming Socrates' skepticism.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6c" id="q6c">
        <label for="q6c">It has eliminated inadequate definitions of piety and clarified what a satisfactory definition would need to look like.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6d" id="q6d">
        <label for="q6d">It has established that Euthyphro's action in prosecuting his father is impious.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Socrates does not claim inquiry is hopeless — he believes there are correct answers. What the elenchus achieves is the elimination of bad answers and progress toward understanding what a good answer would require.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Socrates is not a skeptic about whether piety exists or has a correct definition. The dialogue makes real progress by ruling out inadequate answers. Review Section 4 of the Euthyphro lecture page.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q7b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">7</span> <div><p>When Socrates concludes that he is the wisest person in Athens, what does he mean?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7a" id="q7a">
        <label for="q7a">He possesses special philosophical knowledge that others have not yet acquired.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7b" id="q7b">
        <label for="q7b">He is wiser than others because he alone recognizes that he does not know what he does not know.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7c" id="q7c">
        <label for="q7c">He has studied more widely than any other Athenian and can therefore speak with authority.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7d" id="q7d">
        <label for="q7d">The Oracle's pronouncement must be correct because divine testimony is infallible.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Socratic ignorance is not a claim to superior knowledge — it is an epistemic stance: the refusal to claim certainty you do not have, and the recognition that others' confidence in their knowledge is often unwarranted.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Socrates explicitly denies possessing special knowledge. His advantage is that he alone knows he does not know — while others believe they know things they do not. Review Section 2 of the Apology lecture page.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q8b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">8</span> <div><p>When Socrates investigates the Oracle's claim by questioning politicians, poets, and craftsmen, what pattern does he consistently find?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8a" id="q8a">
        <label for="q8a">Each group possesses genuine wisdom in their own domain, but lacks wisdom about virtue and the good life.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8b" id="q8b">
        <label for="q8b">Each group has a reputation for wisdom that far exceeds their actual understanding — they believe they know things they do not know.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8c" id="q8c">
        <label for="q8c">Each group is genuinely wiser than Socrates in their specific field, disproving the Oracle.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8d" id="q8d">
        <label for="q8d">Each group refuses to engage with Socrates, confirming that wisdom is not communicable.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. In each case Socrates finds vain ignorance: people whose reputation for wisdom is not matched by genuine understanding. The craftsmen come closest — they do possess real skill — but they make the further mistake of thinking their craft gives them wisdom about everything else.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Socrates consistently finds that the reputation for wisdom exceeds actual knowledge. Review Section 2 of the Apology lecture page.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q9b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">9</span> <div><p>When Socrates says "the unexamined life is not worth living," what is he most fundamentally claiming?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9a" id="q9a">
        <label for="q9a">Only professional philosophers live genuinely worthwhile lives.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9b" id="q9b">
        <label for="q9b">A life in which one never reflects on or questions one's own beliefs and values falls short of what fully human existence requires.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9c" id="q9c">
        <label for="q9c">People who do not study philosophy are morally inferior to those who do.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9d" id="q9d">
        <label for="q9d">The soul is immortal, and those who fail to examine their lives will be punished in the afterlife.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Socrates is not claiming only philosophers live well. He is making a claim about rational agency: the capacity to reflect on and question one's beliefs is constitutive of what it means to be a fully human person.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Socrates' claim is about rational agency, not professional philosophy or moral hierarchy. Review Section 4 of the Apology lecture page.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q10b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">10</span> <div><p>In the <em>Apology</em>, Socrates indicates he would accept a verdict of acquittal on the condition that he stop philosophizing in public.</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10a" id="q10a">
        <label for="q10a">True</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10b" id="q10b">
        <label for="q10b">False</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct — this is false. Socrates explicitly states he would disobey such a condition. He would rather face death than abandon his philosophical mission, because ceasing to examine would be worse than death itself.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">False. Socrates says he would refuse this condition even if acquittal depended on it. His commitment to philosophy is unconditional. Review Section 4 of the Apology lecture page.</div>
  </div>
  
  
  
  
  


